       & = \frac{p_{I_1}(C) p_{J_1}(C)}{p_{I_2}(C) p_{J_2}(C)}  = \mu(C)
    \end{align*}
    where the products of the $\lambda$'s cancel out since $I_1\uplus J_1 = I_2 \uplus J_2$.
\end{proof}

This method gives a polynomial-time procedure to obtain an invariant $\mu$ of degree 2 in the Pl\"uckers coordinates.% Now, given a code $C$, we want invariants defined over $C$, i.e. invariants having as denominator a polynomial not vanishing in $C$.

%Let $f\in \mathbb{F}_q[\Grkn]$, we define $U_{f}=\{ C\in \Grkn \mid f(C)\ne 0 \}$, which is an open set in the Zariski topology. In our strategy, we can use only invariants having as denominator a polynomial $f$ such that one of the equivalent codes is in $U_{f}$. Then, given codes $C_1$ and $C_2$, we can pick our invariant from
%\[
%    \left\{ \frac{g}{f} \in \mathbb{F}_q\left(\Grkn \right)^\diagn \mid C_2 \in U_f \right\}.
