diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..6d7cd4232207623b7b711e6324de0ad4868f4f0b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+# Linear code equivalence via Pluecker coordinates (arXiv:2603.09869)
+
+**Build status: locally verified, but not shippable yet.** G1--G8 and the G9 size/effort caps pass. The required bare, structural-hint, and placebo oracle calls all stopped at the API boundary with OpenRouter HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`; errors count as zero attempts, not as model failures. Consequently G9(b) has no verdict and `selftest_report.json` honestly records `all_passed: false`.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B -- no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (the native permutation in one-line form) |
+| Intuition | invariant: balanced products of Pluecker minors cancel basis and column-scale factors |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+The source is Alecci and D'Alconzo, [*Linear Code Equivalence via Pluecker Coordinates*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09869). An instance gives two rank-two generator matrices over `GF(q)`. The solver returns a coordinate permutation `p`; it is valid when one common invertible row-basis change and independent nonzero column scalings carry `G1[:, p[j]]` to `G2[:, j]` for every column. Verification uses exact 2-by-2 determinants: three proposed correspondences fix a projectivity, and all remaining columns must have the same projective coordinates. It never reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse: sample distinct projective points, a random block permutation, two random row bases, and independent nonzero column scales, then form both matrices. Consecutive four-column blocks are identically distributed. Their values
+
+`p01*p23 / (p03*p12)`
+
+are distinct by construction and invariant under both allowed transformations, by the balanced-multiset argument of Section 3, Lemma `prop:IJ`. Section 4, Theorem `th:eqs`, is the paper's bridge from these invariants to equations in the unknown permutation.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is deliberately not a Track A cryptographic-hardness claim. The paper's hard regime has `k = Theta(n)` (the cited LESS parameters have `k >= 126`) and says the full incidence matrix has exponentially many rows. This module fixes `k=2`, following the paper's `Gr(2,4)` running example, so a generic algorithm exists: enumerate an ordered projective frame, extend its unique projectivity, and test the remaining columns. Its worst-case cost is `O(n^4)` field operations. On eight hard instances the implementation solved 8/8, using 1,521,720 counted operations total, a median 220,818 operations per instance, and 0.817 seconds total in the recorded run.
+
+The compact route notices the balanced four-column Pluecker invariant and matches nine labels. It solved 8/8 in 288 exact field operations per hard instance. The paper also warns of easy structure: diagonal-orbit canonicalization is polynomial time, and its introduction reports heuristic polynomial-time recovery for two-sample LCE. This generator uses one sample, but its fixed dimension and planted block invariant still make Track B the only honest label.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=3`)
+
+This is the complete rendered data (the standard output instructions ask for eight comma-separated, zero-based indices):
+
+```text
+q = 101
+G1 row 0: 76 17 59 69 93 46 5 0
+G1 row 1: 6 69 50 74 63 69 41 55
+G2 row 0: 64 7 38 64 53 54 37 63
+G2 row 1: 69 97 62 55 83 45 55 30
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2, 3</answer>`. `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Swapping its first and fifth entries gives `[0, 5, 6, 7, 4, 1, 2, 3]`, which returns `(False, "candidate does not induce one common projective transformation (first failure at target column 3)")`. A person can solve this smallest setting on paper by computing the two balanced block ratios and matching them.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | columns `n` | prime `q` | compact operations | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 101 | 64 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 20 | 1009 | 160 | oracle round could not start |
+| medium | 28 | 10007 | 224 | not reached |
+| hard | 36 | 65537 | 288 | selected shipping preset, pending oracle evidence |
+
+`escalate` raises `q` at fixed answer length before changing any witness-size axis.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged answer recovered from prose/fence; garbage rejected |
+| G4 | pass | 0 hits / 200,000 full-permutation samples; space `36!` |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; demo has exactly one valid answer; baseline 151,908 nodes / 1,521,720 operations over eight instances |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks at 0/8 each; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=72` builds and verifies; fixed-length `q=131071` escalation verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 invariance and 80/80 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 134 characters, 34 estimated tokens, 36 atoms, 288 operations |
+| G9(b) | **not run** | API quota stopped every call; this blocks shipping |
+
+The five failing G6 attacks were raw-column rank matching, affine-coordinate rank matching, a two-anchor affine ansatz, an unbalanced-minor block match, and 256 random block-permutation restarts. Random bases and scales remove the first four signatures; the last still explores a negligible part of the block order space.
+
+## Oracle and G9 evidence
+
+| arm | preset | valid attempts | API errors | solved | conclusion |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 4 | 0 | no hardening verdict |
+| structural hint | hard | 0 | 4 | 0 | no G9(b) verdict |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0 | 4 | 0 | no diagnostic |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo difference is therefore undefined, not zero. The transcript files are script-written error evidence, not hardness evidence. Rerun all three arms after replenishing the OpenRouter key; do not submit or emit this family merely because every attempted call errored.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("gen", "results/2603.09869/gen_2603_09869.py")
+gen = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(gen)
+inst = gen.make_instance(seed=7, **gen.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+answer = gen.parse_answer("<answer>" + ", ".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert gen.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After G9 and the bare hardening loop produce valid `hardened` evidence, emit with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2603.09869 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This distribution is intentionally structured: a solver that tests balanced Pluecker ratios on consecutive groups of four obtains the answer efficiently. Thus 0/200,000 random guesses measures the full-permutation prior stated in the bare problem; it does not estimate the success probability of a solver that has recognized the planted blocks. The family is not evidence for cryptographic security of general LCE, and the small `k=2` regime is easy with tools.
+
+The adversary panel does not include Groebner-basis solving, Beullens/SSA-style codeword search, or a SAT encoding; generic projective-frame enumeration is stronger and complete for these rank-two instances, but implementation constants differ. The canonical key is the unordered four-point `j`-invariant spectrum. It is invariant under all tested relabelings, row bases, scalings, and code swapping, but it is not a complete projective-isomorphism canonical form, so adversarially designed nonisomorphic configurations could collide. Finally, the oracle quota failure leaves the central no-tool claim unmeasured.
