+This is the complete rendered prompt:
 
 ```text
-q = 101
+Linear code equivalence over a prime field
+
+The length is n = 8, and all arithmetic is modulo the prime q = 101. A 2 x 8 generator matrix represents the two-dimensional row subspace that it spans over GF(q).
+Multiplying a generator matrix on the left by an invertible 2 x 2 matrix changes only the chosen row basis. Multiplying an individual column by a nonzero field element is also allowed.
+
+Find a permutation p of the coordinate indices 0,...,n-1 such that there is an invertible 2 x 2 matrix S and there are nonzero scalars d_j with
+    G2[:, j] = d_j * S * G1[:, p[j]]  (mod q)
+for every j. Thus p[j] is the index in G1 corresponding to column j of G2. Order matters, indices are zero-based, and no index may repeat.
+
 G1 row 0: 76 17 59 69 93 46 5 0
 G1 row 1: 6 69 50 74 63 69 41 55
 G2 row 0: 64 7 38 64 53 54 37 63
 G2 row 1: 69 97 62 55 83 45 55 30
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as exactly 8 comma-separated integers.
+Example format (syntax only): <answer>0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
 ```
 
